> 
> I agree: properties in the first place are projections of classes
> (the property of being red is just a reification of the class of red
> things), so reducing properties back to classes doesn't help 

But that is only one possible ontology. In another possibility, 
properties are primitive and classes are derivative. And in that
case, Kinds, notional embodiments of properties, do help.

You are not obliged to embrace this ontology, and propositionalism
allows you to eschew all mention of Kinds. That is, you can
escape intensionality if you choose. 

